WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:05.400
Goosebumps: Haunted Halloween takes us on
a new monster-filled journey with Slappy the

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:08.720
Ventriloquist's Dummy getting up to all kinds
of mischief again!

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:13.190
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here,
and today I'm revealing the secret truth about

00:00:13.190 --> 00:00:18.610
Slappy and Goosebumps 2, including loads of
freaky facts you need to know about the movie!

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:22.730
Make sure you stay to the end to find out
where Goosebumps author RL Stine appears in

00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:27.070
the sequel and hit the bell to keep up-to-date
with all my new videos!

00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:31.210
Slappy the Dummy is one of the most popular
characters in the Goosebumps series.

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:36.610
And although author RL Stine was nearly 50
years old in 1993 when Slappy made his first

00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:40.740
appearance in Night of the Living Dummy, the
inspiration for the creepy puppet actually

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:43.690
goes back many years earlier to Stine's childhood.

00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:48.930
As a young boy, Stine loved listening to his
mother read him The Adventures of Pinocchio,

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:53.120
not the Disney animated version but the original,
much creepier book.

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:57.840
That dark tale of a wooden puppet which comes
to life was Stine's favourite book as a kid

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:00.400
and left a profound mark on him as an adult.

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:04.399
And the classic British horror film, Dead
of Night, which Stine watched as a child and

00:01:04.399 --> 00:01:05.399
which featured a ventriloquist who believed
his dummy was alive and trying to make him

00:01:05.399 --> 00:01:08.750
to commit murder was also hugely important
for Stine's creation of Slappy all those years

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:09.750
later.

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:14.420
By the way, Stine says he enjoys writing books
starring Slappy as he's a really fun character

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:17.260
to write because of how much he insults everyone!

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:22.280
In fact, Stine even has a Slappy dummy in
his office where he creates all his Goosebumps

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:23.280
books!

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:27.960
For the first Goosebumps movie, the filmmakers
wanted Slappy's face to look like actor Jack

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:32.580
Black because not only did Black star in the
film as author RL Stine, but he also did the

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:33.600
voice of Slappy!

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:38.940
However, in Haunted Halloween, Jack Black
only makes a little cameo appearance as Stine.

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:44.229
Indeed, according to the real-life RL Stein,
initially, Black wasn't even in the script

00:01:44.229 --> 00:01:48.869
for the Goosebumps sequel because the actor
was too busy filming another horror movie,

00:01:48.869 --> 00:01:50.619
The House With A Clock In Its Walls.

00:01:50.619 --> 00:01:54.729
Apparently, it wasn't till late in the shoot
that Black found some time in his schedule

00:01:54.729 --> 00:01:58.570
for Goosebumps 2, so a small on-screen part
was created for him.

00:01:58.570 --> 00:02:03.020
All of which means that Jack Black isn't voicing
Slappy this time.

00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:04.080
So, who is?

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:08.509
Well, the new voice of Slappy is provided
by voice actor Mick Wingert, who you may well

00:02:08.509 --> 00:02:13.170
have heard in the animated TV series Kung
Fu Panda: Legends of Awesomeness where he

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:18.620
voiced Po the Panda, another character previously
made famous by none other than Jack Black!

00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:23.769
And it's no wonder the Goosebumps 2 filmmakers
hired Wingert as he does sound uncannily like

00:02:23.769 --> 00:02:24.769
Black!

00:02:24.769 --> 00:02:27.760
"I thought that maybe we should do something
really fun.

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:29.019
"Right, papa?"

00:02:29.019 --> 00:02:32.019
"I love a good holiday sale!"

00:02:32.019 --> 00:02:39.510
In Haunted Halloween, Slappy is absolutely
desperate to be part of a family, so when

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:44.019
things don't work out for him with a human
family called the Quinns, he decides to create

00:02:44.019 --> 00:02:48.269
his own kind of family by bringing all his
monster friends back to life!

00:02:48.269 --> 00:02:49.530
And how does he do that?

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:53.510
Well, he repeats a famous phrase from the
Goosebumps books:

00:02:53.510 --> 00:03:04.799
"Karru Marri Odonna Loma Molonu Karrano!"

00:03:04.799 --> 00:03:09.319
In the books, those magic words mean "You
and I are one now" and when they're spoken,

00:03:09.319 --> 00:03:11.980
they give life to inanimate objects such as
Slappy.

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:16.299
So, it's fun to see Slappy use the phrase
himself in the movie to make a whole variety

00:03:16.299 --> 00:03:19.200
of freaky creatures and ghoulish objects come
alive!

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:24.699
In the sequel, Slappy's physical movements
are controlled on set by a team of puppeteers

00:03:24.699 --> 00:03:28.640
led by Avery Lee Jones, just as they were
in the first film.

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:32.640
And there were actually four different Slappy
puppets used during filming.

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:36.760
The first one was a fully mechanical version
with eyes, neck, and face that could be moved

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:42.529
by remote control and a head that could rotate
all the way round 360 degrees.

00:03:42.529 --> 00:03:47.110
This particular Slappy's legs and arms could
also be moved using a rod, and his fingers

00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:49.790
could be placed in different positions.

00:03:49.790 --> 00:03:54.719
The second Slappy puppet was partially animatronic
and had a mechanical mouth, as well as eyes,

00:03:54.719 --> 00:03:57.840
eyebrows and eyelids that could be moved into
various poses.

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:02.430
There was also a "stunt" puppet that looked
just like the other versions of Slappy except

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:06.739
it had no mechanics and was made completely
of rubber which meant it was light enough

00:04:06.739 --> 00:04:11.090
for the young actors to operate when necessary
during a scene and it was also tough enough

00:04:11.090 --> 00:04:14.529
to withstand any force without breaking during
filming.

00:04:14.529 --> 00:04:19.220
And finally, there was also the original working
ventriloquist doll from the first Goosebumps

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:20.220
movie!

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:25.570
Just like the original film, Haunted Halloween
uses a combination of CGI and practical effects

00:04:25.570 --> 00:04:29.630
including special make-up and costumes to
create the monsters we see on screen.

00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:33.100
Among the creatures that Slappy brings to
life are a bunch of mummies!

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:37.750
And while you may think their costumes look
pretty simple, creating them was really rather

00:04:37.750 --> 00:04:41.600
complicated and they were actually some of
the most elaborate costumes on set.

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:45.440
So, what does making a mummy costume for Goosebumps
2 involve?

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:50.590
Well, first of all, the movie's costume department
cut hundreds of individual pieces of fabric

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:51.970
into strips.

00:04:51.970 --> 00:04:56.660
Then they aged each strip, after which they
sanded them down, and finally, they applied

00:04:56.660 --> 00:05:00.750
those sanded-down, aged strips layer by layer
to form a mummy.

00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:04.900
As there were so many pieces of fabric and
steps involved, each mummy costume took a

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:08.190
team of people more than four days to put
together!

00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:13.000
When Slappy says his magic words to bring
some monster friends to life in the pharmacy,

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:17.780
a mask also gets attached to the store's manager
Walter who turns into an ogre that becomes

00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:18.780
Slappy's sidekick!

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:23.440
Actor Chris Parnell who plays Walter had to
spend more than three hours in make-up to

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:25.330
transform into the ogre.

00:05:25.330 --> 00:05:30.210
That included having a full foam latex cowl
cover his shoulders to the edge of his face,

00:05:30.210 --> 00:05:33.990
as well as having silicone lips and a facial
prosthetic applied.

00:05:33.990 --> 00:05:39.360
And to cover up any exposed skin, a foam silicone
hump and ogre arms were also added.

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:44.730
In the original Goosebumps movie, real-life
author RL Stine had a fun, very meta cameo

00:05:44.730 --> 00:05:49.750
as a drama teacher called Mr Black, who appeared
late on in the film, waving hello to Jack

00:05:49.750 --> 00:05:52.490
Black's fictional RL Stine in the school corridor.

00:05:52.490 --> 00:05:57.090
And, if you're a fan of the real-life Goosebumps
author, then you'll be glad to hear that Stine

00:05:57.090 --> 00:05:58.950
is back for the sequel!

00:05:58.950 --> 00:06:03.050
Make sure you keep a look-out towards the
end of the new film for Stine's blink-and-you'll-miss-it

00:06:03.050 --> 00:06:06.630
cameo appearance as Science Award Presenter,
Principal Harrison!

